The routine atn-m computes the arctangent of a ratio, in whole degrees, by a polynomial of three terms.

Input: n from -999 to 999, standing for the ratio n / 100. Result: atan(n / 100) in whole degrees, rounded,
from -84 to 84.

Call: load n into the accumulator and JMP 22. It returns with the result in the accumulator.
Cells 22-98 (77 cells). It writes cell 99 and, of its own cells, 82-89, 98; nothing else.
Cost: at most 8300 instructions a call, 6704.5 on average over the domain, counted from its first cell to its
return jump. Worst error: 0.573 degrees from the exact value, at n = -675; 86 of the 1,999 inputs are not the
correctly rounded value.
To move the routine down by d cells, subtract d from every address in its instructions that points into
cells 22-98; addresses 00 and 99 stay as they are. It holds no address bases.

